1.53.m:
In this way, through the power of the sun, all the planets, lunar asterisms, and stars
grow and are known, and this entire universe as well;
they do not diminish (na kṣīyante); therefore they are called lunar asterisms (nakṣatra).

Śukra, having sixteen rays and majestic power, entered the abode of Śukra;
and Bṛhaspati, the vast [abode]; and Lohita, the red [abode];
and god Śanaiścara [entered] the abode of Śanaiścara.

Bṛhaspati should be known as less by a fourth than Bhārgava;
Kuja and Saura are both recorded as less by a fourth than Bṛhaspati;
and Budha is less by a fourth than those two in extent and disk.

Five, four, three, and two yojanas
are the disks of the stars, lowest in the east and west;
some are merely half a yojana; smaller than them there is none.

1.55.m:
This right arm of mine is Brahmā, the grandfather of the worlds;
and my left arm is Viṣṇu, who stands ever steadfast in battles.
I am well pleased with you both; I grant a boon as you desire.'
